                "modelAnswer": "a. Describe the mechanism of action of pembrolizumab (3 marks):\n   - Pembrolizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody that binds to the programmed death-1 (PD-1) receptor on T-cells.\n   - It blocks the interaction between PD-1 and its ligands (PD-L1 and PD-L2), thereby restoring anti-tumor T-cell activity.\n   - This enhances the immune system’s ability to recognize and attack tumor cells.\n\nb. List five immune-related adverse events (irAEs) associated with pembrolizumab and their management (12 marks):\n   1. Immune-mediated pneumonitis:\n      - Management: Hold pembrolizumab; administer corticosteroids (e.g., prednisone 1–2 mg/kg/day); consider infliximab if refractory.\n   2. Immune-mediated colitis:\n      - Management: Hold pembrolizumab; administer corticosteroids; consider infliximab if severe.\n   3. Immune-mediated hepatitis:\n      - Management: Hold pembrolizumab; administer corticosteroids; consider mycophenolate if refractory.\n   4. Immune-mediated endocrinopathies (e.g., hypothyroidism, hyperthyroidism):\n      - Management: Hormone replacement therapy (e.g., levothyroxine for hypothyroidism); antithyroid drugs (e.g., methimazole) for hyperthyroidism.\n   5. Immune-mediated dermatitis:\n      - Management: Hold pembrolizumab; administer topical corticosteroids for mild cases; systemic corticosteroids for severe cases.\n\nc. Outline the recommended monitoring schedule for this patient (5 marks):\n   - Baseline: Chest X-ray, liver function tests (LFTs), thyroid function tests (TFTs), cortisol, ACTH.\n   - During therapy: LFTs and TFTs every 4–6 weeks; clinical assessment for signs/symptoms of irAEs at each visit.\n   - If irAEs occur: More frequent monitoring and intervention as above."
