                "modelAnswer": "a) The four classes of antiarrhythmic drugs according to the Vaughan Williams classification are:\n   - Class I: Sodium channel blockers\n   - Class II: Beta-blockers\n   - Class III: Potassium channel blockers\n   - Class IV: Calcium channel blockers\n\n   b) Examples and mechanisms:\n   - Class I: Procainamide – blocks sodium channels, slowing conduction in cardiac tissue.\n   - Class II: Metoprolol – blocks beta-1 adrenergic receptors, reducing heart rate and contractility.\n   - Class III: Amiodarone – blocks potassium channels, prolonging the action potential and refractory period.\n   - Class IV: Diltiazem – blocks calcium channels, reducing conduction velocity in the atrioventricular node."
