                "modelAnswer": "a) The three primary mechanisms by which ACE inhibitors exert their antihypertensive and cardioprotective effects are:\n   - Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE), reducing the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II\n   - Decreased vasoconstriction and aldosterone secretion\n   - Reduced sodium and water retention\n\nb) Examples of ACE inhibitors:\n   - Lisinopril\n   - Enalapril\n\nc) Adverse effects associated with ACE inhibitor use:\n   - Hyperkalemia: ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone secretion, leading to increased serum potassium levels.\n   - Dry cough: ACE inhibitors can cause a persistent dry cough due to increased bradykinin levels."
