              {
                "stem": "A 45-year-old male with a history of type 2 diabetes presents with fever, dysuria, and flank pain. Urine culture grows Escherichia coli resistant to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and nitrofurantoin. Outline the key principles of selecting an appropriate antimicrobial regimen for this patient.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. Assess severity of infection: Pyelonephritis requires systemic therapy; consider hospitalization if severe.\n2. Review local antibiogram: Prefer agents with high susceptibility rates for E. coli in the region.\n3. Consider renal function: Avoid renally eliminated agents or adjust doses if eGFR <30 mL/min.\n4. Choose agents with good tissue penetration: Ceftriaxone achieves high urinary concentrations and is not renally eliminated.\n5. Avoid agents with high resistance potential: Avoid fluoroquinolones if resistance is documented.\n6. Monitor for adverse effects: Consider allergies, drug interactions, and organ toxicity (e.g., ceftriaxone may cause biliary sludging).\n7. Duration of therapy: Typically 7–14 days for pyelonephritis; reassess after culture results."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 30-year-old female with HIV (CD4 150 cells/mm³) presents with fever, cough, and dyspnea. Chest X-ray shows bilateral interstitial infiltrates. Outline the diagnostic approach to confirm Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP).",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Clinical suspicion**: Fever, cough, dyspnea, and bilateral infiltrates in a patient with low CD4 count.\n2. **Laboratory tests**:\n   - **Sputum induction**: Collect induced sputum for direct fluorescent antibody (DFA) staining or PCR for P. jirovecii.\n   - **Bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL)**: Perform if sputum is negative; BAL has high sensitivity (90–95%).\n   - **Blood tests**: LDH often elevated; ABG to assess hypoxia (PaO2 <70 mmHg suggests severe disease).\n3. **Imaging**: Chest X-ray shows bilateral interstitial infiltrates; CT may show ground-glass opacities.\n4. **Diagnosis confirmation**: Identification of P. jirovecii cysts/trophozoites in respiratory specimens via microscopy or PCR."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 50-year-old male with a history of alcohol use disorder presents with fever, cough, and hemoptysis. Chest X-ray shows a right upper lobe cavitary lesion. Sputum smear is positive for acid-fast bacilli. Outline the steps for initiating tuberculosis (TB) treatment in this patient.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Confirm diagnosis**: Sputum smear positive for acid-fast bacilli suggests TB; confirm with GeneXpert or culture.\n2. **Assess for drug resistance**: Perform drug susceptibility testing (DST) if resistance is suspected (e.g., prior TB treatment, contact with resistant cases).\n3. **Baseline investigations**:\n   - Liver function tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin) to assess baseline hepatic function.\n   - Renal function tests (creatinine, eGFR).\n   - HIV serology (TB-HIV co-infection is common).\n   - Baseline audiometry if streptomycin is considered (ototoxicity risk).\n4. **Initiate standard four-drug therapy (RHZE)**:\n   - Isoniazid (INH) 5 mg/kg (max 300 mg) daily.\n   - Rifampicin (RIF) 10 mg/kg (max 600 mg) daily.\n   - Pyrazinamide (PZA) 25 mg/kg (max 2 g) daily.\n   - Ethambutol (EMB) 15–25 mg/kg (max 1.6 g) daily.\n5. **Monitor for adverse effects**:\n   - Hepatotoxicity (monitor LFTs monthly).\n   - Peripheral neuropathy (supplement with pyridoxine 25–50 mg daily).\n   - Visual acuity (for ethambutol).\n6. **Counseling**: Emphasize adherence, importance of completing therapy, and avoiding alcohol (increases hepatotoxicity risk)."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 28-year-old male presents with fever, dysuria, and a purulent urethral discharge. Gram stain shows intracellular gram-negative diplococci. Outline the diagnostic and treatment approach for this patient and his partner.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Diagnosis**:\n   - **Clinical**: Urethritis with purulent discharge and dysuria.\n   - **Laboratory**: Gram stain shows intracellular gram-negative diplococci (suggestive of Neisseria gonorrhoeae); confirm with NAAT or culture.\n   - **Differential**: Consider Chlamydia trachomatis co-infection (perform NAAT for both).\n2. **Treatment for patient**:\n   - **First-line**: Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM single dose (for gonorrhea).\n   - **Chlamydia coverage**: Azithromycin 1 g PO single dose or doxycycline 100 mg PO twice daily for 7 days.\n3. **Partner management**:\n   - **Expedited partner therapy (EPT)**: Provide patient with medication or prescription for partner(s) without prior evaluation.\n   - **Counseling**: Advise abstinence until 7 days after treatment completion and partner treatment.\n4. **Follow-up**:\n   - **Test of cure**: Not routinely required unless symptoms persist or resistance is suspected.\n   - **Screen for other STIs**: HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B/C, and HSV if indicated."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 40-year-old female with a history of chronic hepatitis B presents for evaluation. Her labs show HBsAg positive, anti-HBc positive, anti-HBs negative, and HBV DNA 2,000,000 IU/mL. Outline the goals of therapy and preferred antiviral regimens.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Goals of therapy**:\n   - Suppress HBV replication to prevent progression to cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), and liver failure.\n   - Achieve sustained virologic response (SVR) with undetectable HBV DNA.\n   - Improve liver histology and reduce risk of complications.\n2. **Indications for treatment**:\n   - Elevated ALT (>2x ULN) or evidence of liver inflammation/fibrosis.\n   - HBV DNA >2,000 IU/mL (for HBeAg-negative) or >20,000 IU/mL (for HBeAg-positive).\n3. **Preferred regimens**:\n   - **Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (TDF)**: 300 mg PO daily; high barrier to resistance, renal monitoring required.\n   - **Entecavir**: 0.5–1 mg PO daily; high barrier to resistance, preferred in patients with renal impairment.\n   - **Tenofovir alafenamide (TAF)**: 25 mg PO daily; lower renal toxicity than TDF.\n4. **Monitoring**:\n   - **HBV DNA**: Check at baseline, 12 weeks, and every 6–12 months during therapy.\n   - **LFTs**: Monitor ALT/AST every 3–6 months.\n   - **Renal function**: Monitor creatinine/eGFR every 3–6 months (for TDF/TAF).\n   - **Bone density**: Consider in patients on long-term TDF."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 55-year-old male with a history of COPD presents with fever, productive cough, and dyspnea. Chest X-ray shows a right lower lobe consolidation. Sputum culture grows Streptococcus pneumoniae resistant to penicillin. Outline the principles of antimicrobial stewardship in this case.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Appropriate specimen collection**: Ensure sputum is collected before antibiotics are started to guide therapy.\n2. **Empiric therapy**: Start with a broad-spectrum agent (e.g., ceftriaxone) based on local resistance patterns for CAP.\n3. **De-escalation**: Once culture results are available, switch to a narrower-spectrum agent if possible (e.g., penicillin if susceptible).\n4. **Duration of therapy**: Limit to 5–7 days for CAP unless complications (e.g., empyema) are present.\n5. **Avoid unnecessary antibiotics**: Do not treat colonization or asymptomatic bacteriuria.\n6. **Patient education**: Counsel on completing the full course of antibiotics and avoiding unnecessary future courses.\n7. **Monitor for resistance**: Report resistance patterns to local antibiogram to guide future empiric therapy."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 30-year-old female with a history of HIV (CD4 200 cells/mm³) presents with fever, headache, and confusion. MRI brain shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions. Outline the diagnostic approach and treatment plan for suspected toxoplasmic encephalitis.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Diagnostic approach**:\n   - **Clinical**: Fever, headache, confusion, and focal neurological deficits in a patient with low CD4 count.\n   - **Imaging**: MRI brain shows ring-enhancing lesions (often multiple and bilateral).\n   - **Laboratory**: Toxoplasma IgG positive (suggests prior exposure); PCR for Toxoplasma DNA in CSF if available.\n   - **Differential**: Consider primary CNS lymphoma, tuberculosis, or cryptococcosis.\n2. **Treatment plan**:\n   - **First-line**: Pyrimethamine 200 mg PO loading dose, then 50–75 mg PO daily + sulfadiazine 1–1.5 g PO every 6 hours + leucovorin 10–25 mg PO daily (to prevent bone marrow suppression).\n   - **Alternative**: Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) 10 mg/kg/day (based on TMP) if pyrimethamine is unavailable.\n   - **Duration**: Treat for at least 6 weeks, then consider secondary prophylaxis if CD4 remains <200 cells/mm³.\n3. **Monitoring**:\n   - **Clinical response**: Assess for improvement in symptoms within 10–14 days.\n   - **Adverse effects**: Monitor for bone marrow suppression (leucovorin), rash, and renal toxicity (sulfadiazine).\n   - **Imaging**: Repeat MRI after 2–4 weeks to assess response."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 45-year-old male with a history of IV drug use presents with fever, chills, and a new holosystolic murmur. Blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Outline the management of infective endocarditis (IE) in this patient.",
                "modelAnswer": "1. **Diagnosis**:\n   - **Clinical**: Fever, murmur, and positive blood cultures for S. aureus.\n   - **Echocardiography**: Transthoracic (TTE) or transesophageal (TEE) to assess for vegetations, abscess, or valve dysfunction.\n   - **Duke criteria**: Use to confirm diagnosis (2 major, 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor criteria).\n2. **Treatment**:\n   - **Empiric therapy**: Vancomycin 15 mg/kg IV every 12 hours (for MRSA coverage) + gentamicin 1 mg/kg IV every 8 hours (for synergy) + rifampin 300 mg PO twice daily (for biofilm penetration in prosthetic valves).\n   - **Definitive therapy**: Adjust based on susceptibility results (e.g., nafcillin for MSSA).\n   - **Duration**: 6 weeks for native valve IE; 6 weeks for prosthetic valve IE with gentamicin for first 2 weeks.\n3. **Surgical intervention**:\n   - **Indications**: Heart failure, persistent bacteremia, abscess, or embolic events.\n4. **Monitoring**:\n   - **Blood cultures**: Repeat until negative to confirm clearance of bacteremia.\n   - **Echocardiography**: Repeat to assess response and complications.\n   - **Adverse effects**: Monitor for nephrotoxicity (gentamicin), ototoxicity, and rash (rifampin).\n5. **Counseling**:\n   - **IV drug use cessation**: Refer to addiction services.\n   - **Antimicrobial adherence**: Emphasize completing the full course of therapy."
              }
