                "stem": "A 5-year-old boy from Mwea is brought to the outpatient department. He was well until this morning when he woke up feeling tired and vomited after breakfast. His mother reports he felt hot, so she gave him a half tablet of paracetamol. Examination: well-nourished 20 kg child, alert, not pale, axillary temperature 38.5°C. Social history: recently traveled to Mwea for a family function. Rest of the physical examination is normal.",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Most likely diagnosis and causative agent:\n- Diagnosis: Uncomplicated Plasmodium falciparum malaria\n- Causative agent: Plasmodium falciparum (most likely, given Mwea is a malaria-endemic area in Kenya)\n\nb) Justification for diagnosis:\n1. Travel history: Recent travel to Mwea (malaria-endemic area) for a family function\n2. Clinical presentation: Fever (38.5°C), fatigue, and vomiting are classic for malaria\n3. Age: Children under 5 are at highest risk for severe malaria\n4. Absence of other focal signs (e.g., rash, cough) makes malaria more likely\n5. Mwea is known for rice irrigation schemes which are breeding grounds for Anopheles mosquitoes\n\nc) Three diagnostic tests and their utility:\n1. Thick blood smear: Detects presence of malaria parasites and quantifies parasitemia (more sensitive)\n2. Thin blood smear: Identifies Plasmodium species (important for determining treatment)\n3. Rapid Diagnostic Test (RDT): Quick bedside test detecting HRP2 antigen (specific for P. falciparum)\n4. Complete blood count: May show anemia, thrombocytopenia\n5. Blood glucose: Important baseline before treatment (malaria can cause hypoglycemia)\n\nd) Elaborate treatment plan for this patient:\n1. First-line treatment: Artemether-lumefantrine (Coartem®) 20/120 mg tablets\n   - Dose for 20 kg child: 3 tablets per dose\n   - Administer at 0, 8, 24, 36, 48, and 60 hours (total 6 doses over 3 days)\n   - Take with fatty food to enhance absorption\n2. Alternative: Dihydroartemisinin-piperaquine (Eurartesim®) 20/160 mg tablets\n   - 2 tablets per dose at 0, 24, and 48 hours (total 3 doses)\n   - Take on an empty stomach\n3. Supportive care:\n   - Antipyretics: Paracetamol 15 mg/kg every 6 hours for fever\n   - Adequate hydration: Encourage oral fluids\n   - Monitor for vomiting; repeat dose if vomited within 30 minutes\n4. If vomiting persists or unable to tolerate oral therapy:\n   - Switch to IM artesunate or quinine\n   - Consider hospital admission\n\ne) Three counseling points for the mother:\n1. Complete the full course of antimalarials as prescribed to ensure cure and prevent resistance\n2. Use mosquito bite prevention measures (e.g., insecticide-treated bed nets, repellents) to avoid future infections\n3. Monitor the child for signs of worsening illness (e.g., persistent vomiting, seizures, altered consciousness) and seek medical attention immediately if these occur"
