                "modelAnswer": "f) Most probable diagnosis and justification:\n- Diagnosis: Cerebral malaria (severe P. falciparum malaria with cerebral involvement)\n\nJustification:\n1. Unconsciousness in a child with fever and travel to a malaria-endemic area is highly suggestive of cerebral malaria\n2. Retinal hemorrhages are a classic finding in cerebral malaria\n3. Normal pupillary reflexes help distinguish from other causes of coma\n4. The 'fit' (seizure) is consistent with cerebral involvement\n5. The absence of neck stiffness and rash makes meningitis less likely\n6. The yellowish sticky fluid in the external auditory meatus suggests otitis media, which may be a secondary infection but does not explain the unconsciousness\n7. High fever (40.2°C) and tachycardia (120/min) support systemic infection\n\ng) Appropriate treatment plan for this patient:\n1. Airway and breathing:\n   - Secure airway and ensure adequate oxygenation\n   - Position the child in the recovery position to prevent aspiration\n   - Monitor oxygen saturation continuously\n\n2. IV access and fluids:\n   - Establish IV access for fluids and medications\n   - Avoid overhydration to prevent cerebral edema\n   - Use isotonic fluids (normal saline or Ringer's lactate)\n\n3. Antimalarial therapy:\n   - IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, and 24 hours, then daily until able to tolerate oral therapy\n   - Transition to oral artemether-lumefantrine once stable and able to tolerate fluids\n\n4. Seizure management:\n   - If seizures occur, administer IV or rectal diazepam (0.3–0.5 mg/kg)\n   - Alternative: IV phenobarbital (15–20 mg/kg loading dose)\n   - Avoid phenytoin in malaria due to risk of hypotension and arrhythmias\n\n5. Hypoglycemia prevention:\n   - Check blood glucose frequently\n   - Administer IV dextrose 10% 2–5 mL/kg if glucose <40 mg/dL\n\n6. Supportive care:\n   - Antipyretics for fever\n   - Monitor for complications (anemia, renal failure, pulmonary edema)\n   - Blood transfusion if severe anemia (Hb <5 g/dL)\n\n7. Otitis media:\n   - Administer empirical antibiotics (e.g., IV ceftriaxone) to cover common pathogens\n\n8. Monitoring:\n   - Continuous monitoring of vital signs, neurological status, and urine output\n   - Repeat blood smears every 12–24 hours to assess parasitemia\n   - If parasitemia persists >72 hours, consider alternative therapy\n\n9. Prognosis:\n   - Cerebral malaria has a high mortality rate, particularly in children\n   - Even with prompt treatment, neurological sequelae (e.g., cognitive impairment, epilepsy) may occur\n   - Long-term follow-up is essential to monitor for developmental delays"
