              {
                "stem": "A 50-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer is treated with an aromatase inhibitor (e.g., anastrozole).\n\n(a) Describe the mechanism of action of aromatase inhibitors. (3 marks)\n(b) Outline TWO (2) key adverse effects of aromatase inhibitors. (3 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Mechanism of action of aromatase inhibitors (3 marks): Aromatase inhibitors (e.g., anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane) block the enzyme aromatase, which converts androgens (androstenedione and testosterone) to estrogens (estrone and estradiol) in peripheral tissues (e.g., adipose tissue, breast tissue). By inhibiting aromatase, these drugs reduce circulating estrogen levels, thereby suppressing estrogen-dependent tumor growth in postmenopausal women with hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.\n\nb) Key adverse effects of aromatase inhibitors (3 marks):\n1. Musculoskeletal symptoms: arthralgia, myalgia, and bone pain (common, can be dose-limiting).\n2. Osteoporosis and increased fracture risk: due to estrogen deprivation and reduced bone mineral density (requires bone density monitoring and consideration of bisphosphonates or denosumab).\n3. Vasomotor symptoms: hot flashes and night sweats (due to estrogen withdrawal).\n4. Rare: cardiovascular events (e.g., hypertension, hyperlipidemia) and increased risk of endometrial cancer (lower than with tamoxifen)."
              }
