          {
            "letter": "C",
            "name": "Long Answer Questions",
            "marks": 30,
            "questions": [
              {
                "stem": "D.W, a 48-year old man with a history of hay fever is diagnosed with CKD stage 2. He has been complaining of poor appetite for the last 3 weeks which has seen him lose 2 kgs. The senior house officer calls you regarding a drug that would work for both his hay fever and Appetite.\na) Identify a drug that you would recommend (2 Marks)\nb) Describe the mechanism of action of this drug (2 Marks)\nc) Using suitable examples, examine 2 other different clinical applications of H1 receptor antagonists (2 Marks)\nd) Outline three adverse effects and two drug-drug interactions associated with the drug you identified in (a) above",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Drug recommended for both hay fever and poor appetite: CYPROHEPTADINE (an H1 receptor antagonist with additional 5-HT2 (serotonin) antagonism — it relieves allergic rhinitis and stimulates appetite/weight gain).\n\nb) Mechanism of action:\n• Blocks H1 receptors — relieves the histamine-mediated symptoms of hay fever (sneezing, rhinorrhoea, nasal itching, urticaria).\n• Blocks 5-HT2 receptors in the hypothalamic appetite/satiety centre — reduces serotonergic satiety signalling, increasing appetite and promoting weight gain.\n• Also has anticholinergic and mild sedative (CNS-depressant) actions.\n\nc) Other clinical applications of H1 receptor antagonists (with examples):\n1. Motion sickness and vestibular disorders — dimenhydrinate, meclizine, promethazine (prophylaxis of travel sickness; vertigo).\n2. Sedation/insomnia — diphenhydramine, promethazine (non-prescription sleep aids).\n3. Antiemesis — cyclizine, promethazine (postoperative and chemotherapy-associated nausea).\n4. Common cold — anticholinergic drying of secretions (diphenhydramine/chlorpheniramine in cold remedies).\n5. Adjunct in anaphylaxis — chlorpheniramine (with adrenaline, H2 antagonist and steroids).\n6. Pruritus/urticaria — any H1 antihistamine (hydroxyzine, cetirizine, fexofenadine).\n\nd) Adverse effects of cyproheptadine:\n• Sedation/drowsiness\n• Anticholinergic effects: dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, constipation\n• Dizziness, headache\n• Increased appetite/weight gain (5-HT2 antagonism)\n\nDrug–drug interactions:\n• CNS depressants (alcohol, benzodiazepines, opioids) — additive sedation.\n• MAO inhibitors — enhanced anticholinergic and CNS-depressant effects.\n• Other anticholinergic drugs — additive atropine-like effects.\n• (Cyproheptadine may also antagonise the effects of SSRIs because of 5-HT2 blockade.)"
              },
              {
                "stem": "After careful history taking D.W is also diagnosed with Migraine headaches. He admits that he has been taking a drug which had been prescribed by a neurologist for prophylaxis against his headaches. He is also complaining of dyspnea, tightness and pain in the chest. Examination findings reveal; pleural friction rubs, aortic murmur and pleural effusion. These findings (fibrotic changes in the pleuropulmonary & cardiac regions) are confirmed by chest x-ray and echo.\na) Identify and classify the most likely drug that he was using which is associated with these fibrotic changes (3 Marks)\nb) Outline 3 other adverse effects and 3 contraindications of the drug in (a) above (6 Marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Most likely drug: METHYSERGIDE — classified as an ERGOT ALKALOID and 5-HT2 (serotonin) receptor antagonist used for migraine prophylaxis. It is well known to cause RETROPERITONEAL, PLEUROPULMONARY and CARDIAC VALVULAR FIBROSIS after prolonged use — matching the pleural friction rubs, aortic murmur and pleural effusion (fibrotic pleuropulmonary and cardiac changes) seen in this patient.\n\nb) Other adverse effects of methysergide:\n1. Nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain.\n2. Dizziness, drowsiness, insomnia and hallucinations.\n3. Vasoconstriction — peripheral ischaemia (ergotism), cold extremities, claudication.\n4. Weight gain and oedema.\n5. Muscle cramps, hair loss and weakness.\n(It should be given with 1-month drug-free holidays every 6 months to reduce fibrosis risk.)\n\nContraindications of methysergide:\n1. Pregnancy and lactation.\n2. Peripheral vascular disease / occlusive arterial disease.\n3. Ischaemic heart disease, severe hypertension.\n4. Peptic ulcer disease.\n5. Hepatic or renal impairment.\n6. Connective tissue diseases (fibrotic risk) and valvular heart disease."
              },
              {
                "stem": "AW is a 42-year old patient with advanced liver disease. She is involved in a road traffic accident and sustains a fractured tibia of her left leg. Her eGFR is about 28 mL/min/1.73 m2 (Normal 90 to 120 mL/min/1.73 m2). She is in severe pain and required an analgesic to relieve her pain.\na) Identify a suitable opioid analgesic that you would recommend which would not require dose adjustment and which could be used for induction of anesthesia, maintenance of anaesthesia and analgesic in immediate postoperative period (2 Marks)\nb) List two other opioids that could be considered safe for use in this patient but require dose adjustment (1 Mark)\nc) Outline the mode of action of the drug you identified in (a) above (2 Marks)\nd) Using appropriate examples Examine 3 other clinical applications, 3 adverse effects and 3 contraindications of opioid analgesics (9 Marks)\nAfter surgery, the orthopedic surgeon notices that A.W’s leg is inflamed and thus she requires a short course of an NSAID.\ne) Identify one NSAID considered relatively safe in renal insufficiency that you would recommend (2 Marks)\nf) Outline two drug-drug interactions and two contraindications of the drug you have identified in (e) above (4 Marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Suitable opioid: FENTANYL — a potent synthetic phenylpiperidine opioid that is safe in renal impairment (no active renally-excreted metabolites) and does not require dose adjustment in this patient; it is used for induction of anaesthesia (with a hypnotic), maintenance of anaesthesia, and as an analgesic in the immediate postoperative period.\n\nb) Two opioids safe in renal impairment but requiring dose adjustment:\n• Morphine — active metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide accumulates in renal failure (dose reduction needed).\n• Oxycodone (or hydromorphone, tramadol) — metabolites accumulate in renal impairment; reduce dose and monitor.\n\nc) Mode of action of fentanyl:\nFentanyl is a selective μ-opioid receptor agonist. μ receptors are G protein-coupled (Gi/Go): activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase (↓cAMP), opens K+ channels (membrane hyperpolarisation) and inhibits voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, reducing neurotransmitter (e.g., substance P, glutamate) release from nociceptive afferents in the spinal cord and activating descending inhibitory pathways — producing potent supraspinal and spinal analgesia.\n\nd) Clinical applications of opioid analgesics (with examples):\n1. Severe acute pain — postoperative (fentanyl, morphine), trauma, burns.\n2. Chronic cancer pain — morphine, oxycodone, fentanyl patch.\n3. Myocardial infarction — morphine (reduces pain, anxiety and cardiac work).\n4. Acute pulmonary oedema — morphine (venodilation, reduced preload).\n5. Anaesthesia — fentanyl (analgesic component of balanced anaesthesia).\n6. Cough suppression — codeine (antitussive).\n7. Diarrhoea — loperamide, diphenoxylate.\n8. Obstetric analgesia — pethidine (though neonatal depression is a concern).\n\nAdverse effects of opioids:\n1. Respiratory depression.\n2. Sedation and drowsiness.\n3. Nausea and vomiting.\n4. Constipation (no tolerance develops).\n5. Miosis (pinpoint pupils).\n6. Urinary retention.\n7. Pruritus, flushing.\n8. Tolerance and physical dependence.\n\nContraindications of opioids:\n1. Respiratory depression / severe COPD.\n2. Head injury and raised intracranial pressure.\n3. Undiagnosed acute abdomen.\n4. Biliary colic (spasm of sphincter of Oddi).\n5. Concurrent MAO inhibitors.\n6. Pregnancy (neonatal respiratory depression) and during labour (prolonged).\n7. Infants and breast-feeding (relative).\n\ne) NSAID relatively safe in renal insufficiency: SULINDAC — a prodrug whose renal effects are less pronounced (spares renal prostaglandin synthesis relative to other NSAIDs), making it the classic 'renal-sparing' NSAID choice in mild–moderate renal impairment. (Diclofenac is sometimes cited as an alternative with relatively less renal effect.)\n\nf) Drug–drug interactions of sulindac:\n1. Warfarin/anticoagulants — increased bleeding risk.\n2. ACE inhibitors/ARBs and diuretics — reduced antihypertensive effect and increased risk of renal dysfunction/hyperkalaemia.\n3. Lithium — reduced renal clearance, lithium toxicity.\n4. Methotrexate — reduced excretion, increased toxicity.\n5. Aspirin — additive GI injury; may reduce cardioprotection.\n\nContraindications of sulindac:\n1. Active peptic ulcer disease / GI bleeding.\n2. Severe renal impairment (though relatively renal-sparing, it should be avoided in advanced CKD).\n3. Hypersensitivity to aspirin or other NSAIDs (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease).\n4. Pregnancy (especially third trimester).\n5. Bleeding diathesis."
              }
            ]
          }
