            "questions": [
              {
                "stem": "a) Outline the metabolic pathways of arachidonic acid leading to the synthesis of leukotrienes and prostaglandins. (3 marks)\nb) Explain why leukotriene receptor antagonists are preferred over leukotriene synthesis inhibitors for the prophylaxis of asthma in clinical practice. (2 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Metabolic pathways of arachidonic acid:\n• Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A2 (PLA2).\n• Cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway: Arachidonic acid is converted by COX-1/COX-2 to prostaglandin G2 (PGG2) and then to prostaglandin H2 (PGH2), which serves as a substrate for the synthesis of thromboxane A2 (TXA2), prostaglandins (PGE2, PGF2α, PGI2), and prostacyclin.\n• Lipoxygenase (LOX) pathway: Arachidonic acid is converted by 5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX) to 5-hydroperoxyeicosatetraenoic acid (5-HPETE), which is further metabolized to leukotriene A4 (LTA4). LTA4 is then converted to leukotriene B4 (LTB4) or to cysteinyl leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4).\n\nb) Leukotriene receptor antagonists (e.g., montelukast, zafirlukast) are preferred over leukotriene synthesis inhibitors (e.g., zileuton) for asthma prophylaxis because:\n• They are orally active and have a longer duration of action, allowing once-daily dosing.\n• They have a more favorable safety profile with fewer drug interactions and less risk of hepatotoxicity compared to zileuton, which requires liver function monitoring."
              },
              {
                "stem": "a) Describe the mechanism of action of ergotamine in the treatment of acute migraine. (2 marks)\nb) Explain why ergotamine is contraindicated in patients with peripheral vascular disease. (2 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Mechanism of action of ergotamine in acute migraine:\n• Ergotamine is a partial agonist at 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D receptors.\n• Activation of 5-HT1B receptors on cranial blood vessels causes vasoconstriction of dilated extracerebral vessels, reducing neurogenic inflammation and pain.\n• Activation of 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal nerve endings inhibits the release of pro-inflammatory neuropeptides such as calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP).\n\nb) Ergotamine is contraindicated in peripheral vascular disease because:\n• Ergotamine is a potent vasoconstrictor that can exacerbate peripheral ischemia by reducing blood flow to already compromised extremities.\n• Its α-adrenergic and 5-HT receptor agonism further constricts peripheral blood vessels, increasing the risk of tissue necrosis and gangrene."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 45-year-old patient with a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with acute gout. The physician prescribes colchicine for symptom relief.\na) Explain the mechanism of action of colchicine in acute gout. (2 marks)\nb) Outline two adverse effects of colchicine and their management. (3 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Mechanism of action of colchicine in acute gout:\n• Colchicine binds to tubulin, inhibiting its polymerization into microtubules.\n• This disrupts cytoskeletal function in neutrophils, preventing their migration to the site of inflammation (e.g., joint).\n• It also inhibits the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as interleukin-1β (IL-1β) and leukotriene B4 (LTB4), reducing inflammation and pain.\n\nb) Adverse effects of colchicine and their management:\n1. Gastrointestinal toxicity (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea):\n   • Occurs in up to 80% of patients at therapeutic doses.\n   • Management: Reduce the dose or discontinue the drug; supportive care with antiemetics and hydration.\n2. Myelosuppression (neutropenia, thrombocytopenia):\n   • Rare but serious; dose-related and reversible upon discontinuation.\n   • Management: Monitor complete blood count (CBC) regularly; discontinue colchicine if significant myelosuppression occurs."
              },
              {
                "stem": "a) Compare and contrast the pharmacology of first-generation and second-generation H1 antihistamines. (3 marks)\nb) Provide two clinical scenarios where first-generation H1 antihistamines are preferred over second-generation agents. (2 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Comparison of first-generation and second-generation H1 antihistamines:\n• First-generation H1 antihistamines (e.g., diphenhydramine, promethazine) readily cross the blood-brain barrier, causing sedation and anticholinergic effects (e.g., dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention).\n• Second-generation H1 antihistamines (e.g., loratadine, cetirizine, fexofenadine) have minimal penetration of the blood-brain barrier, resulting in reduced sedation and anticholinergic effects.\n• First-generation agents have a shorter duration of action and require multiple daily dosing, while second-generation agents often have longer durations of action (once-daily dosing).\n\nb) Clinical scenarios where first-generation H1 antihistamines are preferred:\n1. Insomnia: First-generation H1 antihistamines (e.g., diphenhydramine) are used as over-the-counter sleep aids due to their sedative properties.\n2. Motion sickness: First-generation agents with strong anticholinergic effects (e.g., dimenhydrinate, meclizine) are effective for preventing motion sickness."
              },
              {
                "stem": "a) Describe the role of prostaglandins in the regulation of renal function. (3 marks)\nb) Explain why nonselective NSAIDs can exacerbate chronic kidney disease. (2 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Role of prostaglandins in renal function:\n• Prostaglandins (e.g., PGE2, PGI2) are synthesized in the kidney and play a key role in maintaining renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate (GFR).\n• PGE2 and PGI2 vasodilate afferent arterioles, counteracting the vasoconstrictive effects of angiotensin II and norepinephrine.\n• Prostaglandins also regulate sodium and water reabsorption in the renal tubules and modulate renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus.\n\nb) Why nonselective NSAIDs exacerbate chronic kidney disease:\n• Nonselective NSAIDs inhibit both COX-1 and COX-2, reducing the synthesis of vasodilatory prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2).\n• This leads to unopposed vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles, reducing renal blood flow and GFR.\n• In patients with chronic kidney disease, this can precipitate acute kidney injury or accelerate the progression of renal impairment."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 30-year-old patient with a history of severe allergic rhinitis is prescribed a drug that blocks both H1 and 5-HT2 receptors.\na) Identify the drug and explain its mechanism of action. (2 marks)\nb) Outline two additional clinical uses of this drug. (2 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Drug: Cyproheptadine.\nMechanism of action:\n• Cyproheptadine is an H1 antihistamine with additional 5-HT2 receptor antagonism.\n• It blocks H1 receptors, reducing histamine-mediated symptoms such as sneezing, rhinorrhea, and itching.\n• It also blocks 5-HT2 receptors in the hypothalamus, reducing serotonergic satiety signaling and increasing appetite.\n\nb) Additional clinical uses of cyproheptadine:\n1. Appetite stimulation: Used in patients with cachexia or poor appetite (e.g., cancer, HIV/AIDS).\n2. Treatment of carcinoid syndrome: Effective in controlling diarrhea and flushing associated with excess serotonin secretion."
              }
            ]
