            "marks": 40,
            "questions": [
              {
                "stem": "A 60-year old male patient suffering from hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia presents to the hospital.\nWhich drug would you recommend for this patient?(1 Mark)\nWhat is the mode of action of the drugs mentioned in a) above?(2 Marks)\nState 4 side effects of the drug mentioned in a) above.(2 Marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Recommended drug (1 mark): An α1-selective adrenoceptor blocker — e.g., doxazosin, tamsulosin, terazosin or prazosin (a single drug covers both hypertension and BPH).\n\nb) Mode of action (2 marks): Selective competitive blockade of α1-adrenoceptors: (i) on prostatic and bladder-neck smooth muscle → relaxation, improved urinary flow and reduced obstructive symptoms of BPH; (ii) on vascular smooth muscle → arteriolar and venous dilatation, reduced peripheral resistance → lowered blood pressure.\n\nc) Four side effects (2 marks): 1. Postural (orthostatic) hypotension / dizziness; 2. First-dose syncope; 3. Headache; 4. Nasal congestion; also fatigue, weakness, reflex tachycardia, fluid retention."
              },
              {
                "stem": "State any two adjuncts of general anaesthesia that target the autonomic nervous system?(2 Marks)\nWhat is their role in general anaesthesia?(2 Marks)\nWhat is the mode of action of the drugs mentioned in a) above? (4 Marks)\nState any three side effects of each of the drugs mentioned in a) above. (3 Marks\nHow would you manage cases of overdose with the drugs mentioned in a) above?(2 Marks)\nState any four drugs that can be used in treatment of an acute asthmatic attack.(2 Marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Two adjuncts of general anaesthesia that target the autonomic nervous system (2 marks): e.g., 1. Antimuscarinics — atropine or glycopyrrolate (also hyoscine/scopolamine); 2. Sympathomimetic pressor — ephedrine or phenylephrine. (Accept also: β-blockers e.g. esmolol, or α2-agonists e.g. clonidine.)\n\nb) Role in general anaesthesia (2 marks): Antimuscarinics: reduce airway secretions (prevent laryngospasm/aspiration) and prevent vagally mediated bradycardia/reflex bradycardia during intubation and surgery. Pressors: maintain blood pressure and haemodynamic stability during anaesthesia (counteract vasodilation/hypotension caused by anaesthetic agents).\n\nc) Mode of action (4 marks): Atropine/glycopyrrolate — competitive antagonists at muscarinic cholinoceptors, blocking vagal effects on the heart (SA/AV node) and inhibiting glandular secretion. Ephedrine — indirectly acting sympathomimetic: releases noradrenaline from sympathetic nerve terminals and weakly stimulates α- and β-adrenoceptors, raising heart rate, contractility and vascular tone. (If β-blocker chosen: esmolol — selective β1-antagonist; if α2-agonist: clonidine — central α2-agonist reducing sympathetic outflow.)\n\nd) Three side effects of each (3 marks): Atropine — dry mouth, blurred vision/cycloplegia, tachycardia (also urinary retention, constipation). Glycopyrrolate — same antimuscarinic effects with less CNS effect. Ephedrine — hypertension, tachycardia/palpitations, CNS stimulation (insomnia, tremor, anxiety); (esmolol — bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm).\n\ne) Overdose management (2 marks): Antimuscarinic overdose — physostigmine (reversible cholinesterase inhibitor) plus supportive care (cooling, sedation for agitation). Ephedrine/sympathomimetic overdose — discontinue drug, control hypertension/tachycardia with a vasodilator (e.g., phentolamine) and a β-blocker (e.g., propranolol), treat arrhythmias, supportive measures.\n\nf) Four drugs for acute asthma (2 marks): 1. Salbutamol (nebulised/inhaled β2-agonist); 2. Ipratropium bromide (inhaled antimuscarinic); 3. Hydrocortisone IV (or prednisolone oral — systemic corticosteroid); 4. Aminophylline/theophylline IV (methylxanthine); (also oxygen, magnesium sulfate)."
              }
            ]
