                "modelAnswer": "a) Autonomic drug classes for overactive bladder: (3 marks)\n   - Antimuscarinic drugs (first-line)\n   - β3-adrenoceptor agonists\n   - Antimuscarinic drugs with β3 activity (e.g., mirabegron)\n   - Topical estrogen (for postmenopausal women)\n\nb) Example drugs and mechanisms: (6 marks)\n   - Tolterodine (antimuscarinic): Blocks muscarinic (M3) receptors in the detrusor muscle, reducing involuntary contractions and improving bladder storage.\n   - Solifenacin (antimuscarinic): Selectively blocks M3 receptors, reducing urgency, frequency, and urge incontinence.\n   - Mirabegron (β3-agonist): Stimulates β3-adrenoceptors in the detrusor muscle, increasing bladder capacity and reducing overactivity.\n   - Oxybutynin (antimuscarinic): Blocks muscarinic receptors, reducing detrusor overactivity and improving bladder storage.\n\nc) Rationale for antimuscarinic agent in this patient: (3 marks)\n   - Antimuscarinic drugs are first-line therapy for overactive bladder, providing rapid symptom relief.\n   - The patient's glaucoma is a contraindication to mydriatic agents, but antimuscarinic drugs do not cause pupillary dilation when used systemically (unlike topical ophthalmic agents).\n   - Antimuscarinic drugs improve bladder storage and reduce urgency/frequency, enhancing quality of life.\n\nd) Side effects and monitoring: (3 marks)\n   - Dry mouth: Monitor for oral dryness and advise on hydration, sugar-free gum, or saliva substitutes.\n   - Constipation: Assess bowel habits and recommend dietary fiber, hydration, and laxatives if needed.\n   - Blurred vision: Monitor for visual disturbances and advise caution when driving or operating machinery.\n   - Dizziness: Assess for dizziness or postural hypotension, especially in elderly patients."
