              {
                "stem": "Describe ATROPINE under the following headings: (15 marks) (a) Mode of action (2 marks) (b) Five clinical applications (5 marks) (c) Ten side effects (5 marks) (d) Three drug-drug interactions (3 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Mode of action (2 marks): Competitive antagonist at muscarinic (M1–M5) cholinoceptors — reversibly blocks acetylcholine at postganglionic parasympathetic effector sites (heart, smooth muscle, glands, eye) and, being a tertiary amine, also at central muscarinic receptors. No intrinsic agonist activity.\n\nb) Five clinical applications (5 marks):\n1. Pre-anaesthetic medication — reduces salivary/bronchial secretions and prevents vagal bradycardia during intubation.\n2. Bradyarrhythmias — treatment of symptomatic sinus bradycardia and AV block (e.g., after myocardial infarction).\n3. Organophosphate/carbamate insecticide poisoning — reverses muscarinic effects (given with pralidoxime for reactivation of cholinesterase).\n4. Ophthalmic use — mydriasis and cycloplegia for fundoscopy, refraction, and treatment of uveitis/iritis.\n5. Antispasmodic — relief of gastrointestinal, biliary or renal colic.\n6. Antidote to antimuscarinic drug overdose (e.g., atropine-like poisoning from nightshade berries).\n\nc) Ten side effects (5 marks):\n1. Dry mouth; 2. Blurred vision and photophobia (cycloplegia, mydriasis); 3. Tachycardia; 4. Urinary retention; 5. Constipation; 6. Anhidrosis and heat intolerance; 7. Confusion, hallucinations, delirium (central anticholinergic syndrome); 8. Glaucoma exacerbation (angle-closure); 9. Palpitations; 10. Paradoxical bradycardia (with low doses).\n\nd) Three drug-drug interactions (3 marks):\n1. Other anticholinergics (e.g., tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, antihistamines) — additive anticholinergic effects (increased risk of delirium, tachycardia, urinary retention).\n2. Cholinesterase inhibitors (e.g., neostigmine, pyridostigmine) — antagonise atropine’s effects; atropine may be used to counteract cholinergic crisis from cholinesterase inhibitors.\n3. Digoxin — atropine may increase digoxin absorption and toxicity by reducing gastrointestinal motility."
              },
              {
                "stem": "Describe PROPRANOLOL under the following headings: (15 marks) (a) Mode of action (2 marks) (b) Seven clinical applications (7 marks) (c) Six side effects (3 marks) (d) Three drug-drug interactions (3 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Mode of action (2 marks): Nonselective competitive antagonist at β1- and β2-adrenoceptors — blocks catecholamine effects on the heart (β1: reduced rate, contractility, conduction) and on bronchial/vascular smooth muscle and metabolic tissues (β2). Membrane-stabilising effect only at high doses.\n\nb) Seven clinical applications (7 marks):\n1. Hypertension — reduces cardiac output and renin release.\n2. Angina pectoris — reduces myocardial oxygen demand.\n3. Post-myocardial infarction — secondary prevention (reduces mortality and arrhythmias).\n4. Cardiac arrhythmias — supraventricular tachyarrhythmias, rate control in atrial fibrillation/flutter.\n5. Heart failure — compensated stable HFrEF (initiated cautiously, uptitrated under specialist supervision).\n6. Migraine prophylaxis — reduces frequency and severity of attacks.\n7. Essential tremor — symptomatic control.\n8. Thyrotoxicosis/hyperthyroidism — controls tachycardia, tremor, and other adrenergic symptoms.\n9. Portal hypertension — reduces portal venous pressure in cirrhosis.\n\nc) Six side effects (3 marks):\n1. Bradycardia and heart block; 2. Hypotension; 3. Bronchospasm (β2 blockade); 4. Fatigue and lethargy; 5. Cold extremities; 6. Masking of hypoglycaemia symptoms (tremor, tachycardia) in diabetics.\n\nd) Three drug-drug interactions (3 marks):\n1. Calcium channel blockers (e.g., verapamil, diltiazem) — additive negative inotropic and chronotropic effects, risk of severe bradycardia, heart failure, or asystole.\n2. Digoxin — additive bradycardic effect; increased risk of AV block.\n3. Insulin or oral hypoglycaemics — propranolol masks tachycardia and tremor, delaying recognition of hypoglycaemia; may prolong hypoglycaemia by inhibiting glycogenolysis."
              }
