                "stem": "Describe five mechanisms through which bacteria develop resistance to antibiotics. Provide one example for each mechanism. (7 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Five mechanisms of bacterial antibiotic resistance:\n1. ENZYMATIC INACTIVATION: Bacteria produce enzymes that destroy or modify the antibiotic. Example: beta-lactamases hydrolyze the beta-lactam ring of penicillins/cephalosporins (e.g., TEM-1 beta-lactamase in E. coli).\n2. TARGET MODIFICATION: Alteration of the antibiotic target site reduces binding affinity. Example: MRSA acquires mecA gene encoding PBP2a, a penicillin-binding protein with low affinity for beta-lactams.\n3. DECREASED PERMEABILITY: Reduced entry of the antibiotic into the bacterial cell. Example: loss of porins (OprD) in Pseudomonas aeruginosa reduces imipenem entry.\n4. ACTIVE EFFLUX: Efflux pumps expel antibiotics from the bacterial cell. Example: tetracycline efflux pumps (TetA/B) in gram-negative bacteria pump tetracyclines out of the cell.\n5. BYPASS PATHWAYS: Bacteria develop alternative metabolic pathways to bypass the inhibited step. Example: vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE) alter peptidoglycan precursors from D-ala-D-ala to D-ala-D-lac, reducing vancomycin binding."
