              {
                "stem": "A 28-year-old female sex worker presents with dysuria, urinary frequency, and purulent vaginal discharge. Gram stain reveals Gram-negative diplococci. She is diagnosed with gonococcal urethritis. (a) Outline the recommended treatment regimen for gonococcal urethritis, including rationale for dual therapy. (5 marks) (b) Describe the pharmacology of ceftriaxone, including mechanism of action, spectrum of activity, and key adverse effects. (5 marks) (c) Explain the importance of partner notification and follow-up in this case. (5 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Recommended treatment regimen:\n   - Ceftriaxone 250 mg IM single dose (first-line for gonococcal infections due to rising ciprofloxacin resistance).\n   - Azithromycin 1 g oral single dose (to cover possible co-infection with Chlamydia trachomatis, which is common in gonococcal urethritis).\n   - Rationale for dual therapy: Gonococcal infections are often co-infected with Chlamydia trachomatis, and azithromycin provides effective coverage for chlamydia. Dual therapy also reduces the risk of antimicrobial resistance.\n\nb) Pharmacology of ceftriaxone:\n   - Mechanism of action: Ceftriaxone is a third-generation cephalosporin that inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), leading to cell lysis and death.\n   - Spectrum of activity: Broad-spectrum activity against Gram-negative bacteria (including Neisseria gonorrhoeae) and some Gram-positive bacteria. Limited activity against anaerobic bacteria.\n   - Key adverse effects: Hypersensitivity reactions (rash, anaphylaxis), injection site pain, diarrhea, and rarely Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea. Ceftriaxone is generally well-tolerated but should be avoided in patients with severe penicillin/cephalosporin allergy.\n\nc) Importance of partner notification and follow-up:\n   - Partner notification: Essential to prevent reinfection and further transmission. All sexual partners in the past 60 days should be notified, tested, and treated empirically for gonorrhea and chlamydia to break the chain of transmission.\n   - Follow-up: Re-testing for gonorrhea and chlamydia at 3 months post-treatment is recommended to detect reinfection or treatment failure. Patient education on safe sex practices, condom use, and regular STI screening is critical.\n   - Public health impact: Partner notification and follow-up reduce the prevalence of gonorrhea and chlamydia in the community, preventing complications such as PID, infertility, and disseminated gonococcal infection."
              }
