              {
                "stem": "A 50-year-old male with a 20-year history of type 2 diabetes mellitus presents with poor glycaemic control despite taking metformin 1 g twice daily and glibenclamide 5 mg twice daily. His HbA1c is 9.2%, and his BMI is 32 kg/m². (a) Discuss the stepwise approach to intensifying his diabetes therapy, including the rationale for each step (10 marks). (b) Outline the monitoring plan for this patient following therapy intensification (5 marks).",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Stepwise approach to intensifying diabetes therapy (10 marks):\n1. Assess adherence and lifestyle factors: Ensure metformin and glibenclamide are taken correctly; review diet, physical activity, and weight management.\n2. Optimise metformin: Increase dose to 2 g/day if tolerated (maximal effective dose), or switch to extended-release formulation to reduce GI side effects.\n3. Review sulfonylurea: Consider switching glibenclamide to a longer-acting sulfonylurea (e.g., glimepiride) or a safer alternative (e.g., gliclazide) to reduce hypoglycaemia risk.\n4. Add a second-line oral agent:\n   - SGLT2 inhibitor (e.g., empagliflozin): Reduces hyperglycaemia by increasing urinary glucose excretion; also provides weight loss and cardiovascular benefits.\n   - DPP-4 inhibitor (e.g., sitagliptin): Enhances incretin effect, reducing postprandial glucose; weight-neutral.\n   - Thiazolidinedione (e.g., pioglitazone): Improves insulin sensitivity; however, consider weight gain and oedema risks.\n5. Consider injectable therapy if HbA1c remains >7.5%:\n   - GLP-1 receptor agonist (e.g., liraglutide): Enhances glucose-dependent insulin secretion, suppresses glucagon, slows gastric emptying, and promotes weight loss.\n   - Basal insulin (e.g., insulin glargine): Provides consistent background insulin coverage; start with low dose and titrate based on fasting glucose.\n6. Combination therapy: Consider fixed-dose combinations (e.g., metformin + SGLT2 inhibitor or metformin + DPP-4 inhibitor) to improve adherence.\n7. Address comorbidities: Treat hypertension (ACE inhibitor/ARB), dyslipidaemia (statin), and consider aspirin for primary prevention if high cardiovascular risk.\n\nRationale: Stepwise intensification aims to achieve glycaemic targets while minimising hypoglycaemia, weight gain, and adverse effects. Choice of agent depends on patient factors (e.g., renal function, cardiovascular risk, cost, and preferences).\n\nb) Monitoring plan following therapy intensification (5 marks):\n1. Glycaemic control: HbA1c every 3 months until target achieved, then every 6 months; fasting plasma glucose and 2-hour postprandial glucose as needed.\n2. Renal function: Serum creatinine and eGFR at baseline and every 6–12 months (especially with SGLT2 inhibitors or metformin).\n3. Liver function: LFTs if starting pioglitazone or GLP-1 receptor agonist.\n4. Weight and BMI: Monitor every 3–6 months; assess for weight loss or gain.\n5. Blood pressure: Monitor every 3–6 months; titrate antihypertensives as needed.\n6. Lipid profile: Baseline and annually or as indicated.\n7. Hypoglycaemia: Educate patient on symptoms and management; monitor for recurrent hypoglycaemia.\n8. Adverse effects: Monitor for GI symptoms (GLP-1 agonists), urinary tract infections (SGLT2 inhibitors), oedema (pioglitazone), or injection-site reactions (insulin/GLP-1 agonists).\n9. Cardiovascular risk: Assess for symptoms of heart failure (pioglitazone) or pancreatitis (GLP-1 agonists).\n10. Patient education: Reinforce adherence, diet, exercise, and self-monitoring of blood glucose."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 65-year-old female with a history of COPD (GOLD Stage 3) presents with worsening dyspnoea, increased sputum production, and a cough productive of yellow-green sputum over the past 5 days. She is currently on tiotropium bromide 18 mcg daily and salbutamol as needed. Her oxygen saturation is 88% on room air, and her chest X-ray shows no consolidation. (a) Discuss the pharmacological management of this acute exacerbation of COPD (10 marks). (b) Outline the non-pharmacological strategies to prevent future exacerbations (5 marks).",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Pharmacological management of acute exacerbation of COPD (10 marks):\n1. Oxygen therapy: Administer controlled oxygen (24–28% via Venturi mask) to maintain SpO2 88–92% (avoid hyperoxia to prevent CO2 retention).\n2. Bronchodilators:\n   - Short-acting beta-2 agonist (SABA): Nebulised salbutamol 2.5–5 mg every 4–6 hours as needed.\n   - Short-acting muscarinic antagonist (SAMA): Nebulised ipratropium bromide 500 mcg every 6 hours (additive bronchodilation with SABA).\n3. Corticosteroids: Oral prednisolone 30 mg daily for 5–7 days to reduce airway inflammation and speed recovery (avoid prolonged courses to minimise adverse effects).\n4. Antibiotics: Empirical therapy for 5–7 days if purulent sputum or signs of infection (e.g., amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily, or doxycycline 200 mg on day 1 then 100 mg daily if penicillin-allergic).\n5. Consider additional therapies if severe exacerbation:\n   - Theophylline (oral or IV) if inadequate response to bronchodilators (monitor serum levels due to narrow therapeutic index).\n   - Consider noninvasive ventilation (NIV) if respiratory acidosis (pH <7.35, PaCO2 >6.0 kPa) or severe dyspnoea.\n6. Monitor for complications: Respiratory failure, arrhythmias, or heart failure.\n\nb) Non-pharmacological strategies to prevent future exacerbations (5 marks):\n1. Smoking cessation: The most important intervention to slow COPD progression; provide behavioural support and pharmacotherapy (nicotine replacement, varenicline, or bupropion).\n2. Pulmonary rehabilitation: Supervised exercise programme to improve exercise capacity, reduce dyspnoea, and enhance quality of life.\n3. Vaccinations: Annual influenza vaccine and pneumococcal vaccine (PPSV23) to reduce risk of respiratory infections.\n4. Self-management education: Teach patients to recognise early signs of exacerbation (increased sputum, change in colour, worsening dyspnoea) and initiate action plans (e.g., increase bronchodilator use, start oral corticosteroids/antibiotics).\n5. Nutritional support: Address malnutrition or obesity; consider dietary counselling for weight management.\n6. Avoidance of triggers: Reduce exposure to air pollution, occupational dusts, and indoor biomass fuels.\n7. Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT): If chronic hypoxaemia (PaO2 <7.3 kPa) to improve survival and reduce hospitalisations.\n8. Regular follow-up: Monitor lung function (spirometry), symptoms (COPD Assessment Test, CAT), and adherence to therapy; adjust treatment as needed."
              }
