                "stem": "A 40-year-old female presents with a 2-week history of productive cough, fever, and night sweats. Sputum microscopy and culture confirm pulmonary tuberculosis. She is started on standard anti-tuberculosis therapy: rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol.\n\n(a) Explain the role of acetylation in the metabolism of isoniazid and its clinical implications (6 marks).\n(b) Describe the mechanism by which rifampicin affects the metabolism of co-administered drugs (5 marks).\n(c) Outline the monitoring plan for this patient to detect and manage adverse effects related to these drugs (4 marks).",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Role of acetylation in isoniazid metabolism: Isoniazid is metabolised by N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2) in the liver to acetylisoniazid, which is further hydrolysed to inactive metabolites. The rate of acetylation is genetically determined: rapid acetylators metabolise isoniazid quickly, while slow acetylators accumulate the drug and its hepatotoxic metabolites. Clinical implications: Rapid acetylators may require higher doses to achieve therapeutic levels but are at increased risk of drug-induced hepatitis due to higher metabolite formation. Slow acetylators are more prone to peripheral neuropathy due to accumulation of isoniazid and reduced pyridoxine (vitamin B6) availability.\n\nb) Mechanism of rifampicin’s effect on drug metabolism: Rifampicin is a potent inducer of cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP3A4, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19, as well as UDP-glucuronosyltransferases (UGTs). It binds to and activates the pregnane X receptor (PXR), leading to increased transcription of drug-metabolising enzymes. This results in enhanced metabolism and reduced plasma concentrations of co-administered drugs that are substrates for these enzymes.\n\nc) Monitoring plan:\n- Liver function tests (LFTs): Baseline and monthly monitoring for hepatotoxicity (e.g., elevated ALT/AST, bilirubin) due to isoniazid and rifampicin. Discontinue if ALT >3 times upper limit of normal with symptoms or >5 times without symptoms.\n- Peripheral neuropathy: Assess for numbness, tingling, or weakness monthly; supplement with pyridoxine (vitamin B6) 25-50 mg daily to prevent neuropathy.\n- Visual acuity and colour vision: Baseline and monthly for ethambutol to detect optic neuritis.\n- Renal function: Monitor serum creatinine and electrolytes, particularly if the patient has risk factors for renal impairment.\n- Symptoms: Instruct the patient to report jaundice, dark urine, abdominal pain, or visual disturbances.\n- Drug interactions: Review concurrent medications for CYP3A4 substrates (e.g., oral contraceptives, antiretrovirals) and adjust doses or consider alternative therapies as needed."
