              {
                "stem": "1. A 60-year-old male presents to your pharmacy with a prescription for a branded drug that is not in stock. You decide to substitute it with a generic equivalent that is available. The patient is confused and asks for clarification on the differences between branded and generic drugs.\n\n   a) Explain the difference between a branded drug and a generic drug, including their regulatory requirements and bioequivalence (8 marks).\n   b) Define the term 'chemical name' of a drug (2 marks).\n   c) Discuss five ideal properties of a drug (5 marks).",
                "modelAnswer": "a) Branded vs. Generic Drugs:\n- Branded drugs: Developed and marketed by a pharmaceutical company under a proprietary name (e.g., Lipitor for atorvastatin). They are protected by patents for a period (typically 20 years) during which no other company can market the same drug. Branded drugs undergo extensive clinical trials to establish safety, efficacy, and quality before regulatory approval.\n- Generic drugs: Copies of branded drugs that are marketed after the patent expires. They contain the same active pharmaceutical ingredient (API) as the branded drug and are required to demonstrate bioequivalence (similar rate and extent of absorption) to the branded drug. Generic drugs are generally less expensive due to reduced development costs and competition.\nRegulatory requirements for generics include proof of bioequivalence, adherence to Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP), and compliance with pharmacopoeial standards.\n\nb) Chemical name: The chemical name of a drug is a precise description of its molecular structure, based on chemical nomenclature rules (e.g., N-(4-hydroxyphenyl)acetamide for paracetamol). It is not used in clinical practice but is important for chemical identification and synthesis.\n\nc) Five ideal properties of a drug:\n1) Effectiveness: The drug should produce the desired therapeutic effect in the target population.\n2) Safety: The drug should have a favorable benefit-risk ratio, with minimal adverse effects at therapeutic doses.\n3) Selectivity: The drug should act on specific targets (e.g., receptors, enzymes) to minimize off-target effects.\n4) Predictability: The drug's pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics should be consistent and predictable across patients.\n5) Chemical stability: The drug should be stable under normal storage conditions to maintain potency and safety."
              },
              {
                "stem": "2. Discuss the sources of drugs, providing examples for each source (15 marks).",
                "modelAnswer": "Sources of Drugs:\n\n1) Natural Sources:\n   - Plants: Many drugs are derived from plant sources, such as morphine (from Papaver somniferum), digoxin (from Digitalis lanata), and atropine (from Atropa belladonna). These drugs are often extracted from plant parts (e.g., leaves, roots, bark) and purified for clinical use.\n   - Animals: Some drugs are obtained from animal sources, such as insulin (from porcine or bovine pancreas), heparin (from porcine intestinal mucosa), and thyroid hormones (from animal thyroid glands).\n   - Microorganisms: Antibiotics and other drugs are derived from microorganisms, such as penicillin (from Penicillium fungi), streptomycin (from Streptomyces bacteria), and cyclosporine (from Tolypocladium fungi).\n   - Minerals: Inorganic compounds are used as drugs, such as lithium (for bipolar disorder), magnesium sulfate (as a laxative and anticonvulsant), and iron (for anemia).\n\n2) Synthetic Sources:\n   - Many drugs are synthesized chemically in the laboratory, such as aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), paracetamol (acetaminophen), and most modern antibiotics (e.g., ciprofloxacin). Synthetic drugs are designed to mimic natural compounds or to have specific pharmacological properties.\n\n3) Semi-synthetic Sources:\n   - These drugs are derived from natural sources but are chemically modified to improve their properties. Examples include:\n     - Amoxicillin (derived from penicillin but modified to improve oral absorption).\n     - Heroin (diacetylmorphine, derived from morphine but modified to increase lipid solubility).\n     - Ethinylestradiol (a synthetic derivative of estradiol used in oral contraceptives).\n\n4) Biotechnology/Recombinant DNA Technology:\n   - Modern biotechnology has enabled the production of drugs using recombinant DNA technology. Examples include:\n     - Insulin (human insulin produced by genetically modified bacteria or yeast).\n     - Erythropoietin (used to treat anemia, produced by recombinant DNA technology).\n     - Monoclonal antibodies (e.g., infliximab, used in autoimmune diseases).\n\n5) Marine Sources:\n   - Some drugs are derived from marine organisms, such as:\n     - Ziconotide (Prialt®, derived from a marine cone snail, used for chronic pain).\n     - Cytarabine (a chemotherapy drug derived from a Caribbean sea sponge).\n\n6) Synthetic Peptides and Proteins:\n   - Peptides and proteins can be synthesized chemically or produced using recombinant DNA technology. Examples include:\n     - Oxytocin (a peptide hormone used to induce labor).\n     - Growth hormone (somatropin, used to treat growth disorders).\n\nEach source has its advantages and limitations, and the choice of source depends on factors such as availability, cost, efficacy, and safety."
              }
