                "stem": "Give six approaches that could be used in the discovery of drugs (6 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "SIX APPROACHES TO DRUG DISCOVERY (6 marks) (any six):\n1. RANDOM SCREENING of natural products, plant extracts and compound libraries (e.g., penicillin from Penicillium mold).\n2. MODIFICATION/ANALOG DESIGN of existing drugs to improve efficacy, safety or pharmacokinetics (me-too drugs; e.g., cephalosporins from penicillin nucleus).\n3. RATIONAL DRUG DESIGN based on knowledge of the molecular target/receptor structure (structure-based design; enzyme/receptor inhibitors).\n4. COMPUTER-AIDED/MOLECULAR MODELING (in silico drug design) — virtual screening, docking, QSAR.\n5. EXPLOITATION OF PHYSIOLOGICAL/BIOCHEMICAL PATHWAYS — studying hormones, neurotransmitters and mediators as leads (e.g., beta-blockers from catecholamines).\n6. SERENDIPITY (accidental discovery) — e.g., penicillin, chlorpromazine, sildenafil.\n7. COMBINATORIAL CHEMISTRY + HIGH-THROUGHPUT SCREENING of large compound libraries.\n8. BIOTECHNOLOGY/GENETIC ENGINEERING — recombinant proteins, monoclonal antibodies (e.g., insulin, trastuzumab).\n9. ETHNOPHARMACOLOGY/TRADITIONAL MEDICINE — screening of herbal remedies (e.g., artemisinin from Artemisia annua, quinine from cinchona).\n10. PRODRUG DESIGN — improving delivery of an active compound.\n(Any six.)"
