              {
                "stem": "Describe five key stages in the drug discovery and development pipeline, from target identification to post-marketing surveillance. (5 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "FIVE KEY STAGES IN DRUG DISCOVERY AND DEVELOPMENT (5 marks):\n1. TARGET IDENTIFICATION: Identification of a biological target (e.g., enzyme, receptor, pathway) linked to a disease using genomics, proteomics, or bioinformatics.\n2. TARGET VALIDATION: Confirmation that modulating the target will produce a therapeutic effect (e.g., knockout/knockdown studies, animal models).\n3. LEAD DISCOVERY: Identification of lead compounds (e.g., via high-throughput screening, virtual screening, or natural product screening) that interact with the target.\n4. LEAD OPTIMIZATION: Modification of lead compounds to improve potency, selectivity, pharmacokinetics, and reduce toxicity (e.g., structure-activity relationship studies, prodrug design).\n5. PRECLINICAL DEVELOPMENT: GLP-compliant toxicology and pharmacology studies in animals to assess safety, efficacy, and pharmacokinetics before human trials.\n6. CLINICAL DEVELOPMENT: Phases 1-4 trials in humans to assess safety, tolerability, efficacy, and post-marketing surveillance for adverse effects.\n7. REGULATORY APPROVAL: Submission of data to regulatory agencies (e.g., PPB, WHO) for marketing authorization."
              },
              {
                "stem": "List four key principles of toxicokinetics and briefly explain their relevance in clinical toxicology. (4 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "FOUR KEY PRINCIPLES OF TOXICOKINETICS (4 marks):\n1. ABSORPTION: Rate and extent of toxicant entry into the bloodstream (e.g., oral, inhalation, dermal). Relevance: Determines time to peak toxicity and choice of decontamination (e.g., activated charcoal for oral ingestion).\n2. DISTRIBUTION: Tissue distribution of the toxicant (e.g., lipid-soluble toxins distribute to fat, heavy metals to bone). Relevance: Predicts target organs (e.g., lead to CNS/bone marrow; organophosphates to synapses).\n3. METABOLISM: Biotransformation of the toxicant (e.g., phase I/II reactions). Relevance: May activate (e.g., paracetamol to NAPQI) or detoxify (e.g., methanol to formic acid) the toxicant; influences antidote choice.\n4. ELIMINATION: Excretion of the toxicant or metabolites (renal, biliary, pulmonary). Relevance: Determines half-life and need for enhanced elimination (e.g., hemodialysis for methanol, forced diuresis for salicylates)."
              },
              {
                "stem": "A 3-year-old child is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after ingesting an unknown number of iron tablets. Outline the immediate management steps, including antidote therapy. (6 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "IMMEDIATE MANAGEMENT OF IRON POISONING IN A 3-YEAR-OLD (6 marks):\n1. ASSESSMENT AND STABILIZATION: ABCDE approach; check vital signs, mental status, and airway patency. Administer oxygen if hypoxic.\n2. DECONTAMINATION: Administer activated charcoal (1 g/kg) if ingestion was recent (<1 hour) and the child is alert. Avoid ipecac syrup (no longer recommended).\n3. ANTIDOTE THERAPY: Administer DEFEROXAMINE (iron chelator) intravenously if:\n   - Serum iron level >500 mcg/dL OR\n   - Severe symptoms (e.g., vomiting, diarrhea, lethargy, shock) OR\n   - Radiopaque tablets visible on X-ray.\n   Dose: 15 mg/kg/hour IV infusion (maximum 80 mg/kg/day). Monitor for hypotension and allergic reactions.\n4. SUPPORTIVE CARE: IV fluids for shock/hypotension; monitor for metabolic acidosis, hepatic failure, and coagulopathy.\n5. MONITORING: Serial serum iron levels, CBC, LFTs, renal function, and glucose.\n6. ADMISSION: Admit for observation and monitoring; consider ICU if severe poisoning."
              },
              {
                "stem": "Describe the mechanism of action, clinical use, and adverse effects of N-acetylcysteine in paracetamol poisoning. (7 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "N-ACETYLCYSTEINE IN PARACETAMOL POISONING (7 marks):\nMECHANISM OF ACTION:\n- N-acetylcysteine (NAC) acts as a precursor for glutathione synthesis.\n- Glutathione detoxifies NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine), the toxic metabolite of paracetamol, by conjugation.\n- NAC also scavenges free radicals and improves oxygen delivery.\n\nCLINICAL USE:\n- Indicated for paracetamol overdose when:\n  - Plasma paracetamol level is above the treatment line on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram (e.g., >100 mg/L at 4 hours post-ingestion).\n  - Time of ingestion is unknown and serum level cannot be plotted.\n  - Features of liver toxicity (e.g., elevated ALT/AST, INR) even if paracetamol level is below treatment line.\n- Route: Oral (loading dose 140 mg/kg, then 70 mg/kg every 4 hours for 17 doses) or IV (150 mg/kg over 1 hour, then 50 mg/kg over 4 hours, then 100 mg/kg over 16 hours).\n\nADVERSE EFFECTS:\n- Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea (common with oral NAC).\n- Anaphylactoid reactions (flushing, rash, pruritus, bronchospasm, hypotension) — more common with IV NAC.\n- Rare: Severe allergic reactions (e.g., angioedema, shock).\n- Monitor for hepatotoxicity (elevated ALT/AST, INR) and renal failure."
              },
              {
                "stem": "a) Define the term 'therapeutic index' and explain its clinical significance. (3 marks)\nb) How does the therapeutic index differ from the 'safety margin'? (2 marks)\nc) Give one example of a drug with a narrow therapeutic index. (1 mark)",
                "modelAnswer": "a) THERAPEUTIC INDEX (3 marks):\n- Definition: The ratio of the toxic dose (TD50) to the effective dose (ED50) in a population: TI = TD50/ED50.\n- Clinical significance: A low therapeutic index indicates a narrow margin between therapeutic and toxic doses, requiring careful dose titration, therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM), and close patient monitoring to avoid toxicity.\n\nb) THERAPEUTIC INDEX VS. SAFETY MARGIN (2 marks):\n- Therapeutic index is a population-based ratio (TD50/ED50).\n- Safety margin is the difference between the maximum tolerated dose and the minimum effective dose in an individual patient, reflecting the practical dosing range in clinical use.\n\nc) EXAMPLE OF A DRUG WITH NARROW THERAPEUTIC INDEX (1 mark):\n- Warfarin (TI ~1.5-2)\n- Digoxin (TI ~2)\n- Lithium (TI ~2-3)\n- Theophylline (TI ~2-3)"
              },
              {
                "stem": "Outline the ABCDE approach to the management of a patient with acute poisoning. Provide one example of a toxicant for which each step is particularly critical. (8 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "ABCDE APPROACH TO ACUTE POISONING (8 marks):\nA (AIRWAY): Ensure airway patency; intubate if necessary (e.g., opioid overdose with respiratory depression).\nB (BREATHING): Assess respiratory rate, oxygen saturation; administer oxygen if hypoxic (e.g., carbon monoxide poisoning).\nC (CIRCULATION): Check pulse, blood pressure; administer IV fluids if hypotensive (e.g., iron poisoning with shock).\nD (DISABILITY): Assess mental status (GCS); consider antidotes for altered consciousness (e.g., naloxone for opioid overdose, flumazenil for benzodiazepine overdose).\nE (EXPOSURE): Remove contaminated clothing; decontaminate skin/eyes if dermal exposure (e.g., organophosphate poisoning with dermal absorption).\n\nEXAMPLES:\n- Opioid overdose: Critical at A (airway protection) and D (naloxone for respiratory depression).\n- Carbon monoxide poisoning: Critical at B (oxygen therapy for hypoxia).\n- Iron poisoning: Critical at C (IV fluids for shock).\n- Organophosphate poisoning: Critical at E (decontamination for dermal exposure)."
              }
