            "questions": [
              {
                "stem": "Explain the major species differences in drug pharmacokinetics using THREE of the following examples: cats, horses, ruminants and pigs. (6 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Any three of the following (2 marks each):\n1. CATS - Defective glucuronidation: cats have low hepatic glucuronosyltransferase, so drugs such as paracetamol, aspirin and morphine are poorly conjugated and either become toxic (paracetamol - NAPQI accumulation) or are eliminated slowly. Phenolic drugs are especially hazardous.\n2. HORSES - Cannot vomit: gastric anatomy prevents emesis, so emetics are useless; oral intoxications are managed by gastric lavage/tubing. Horses also have a large caecum where oral drugs may be metabolised by microflora.\n3. RUMINANTS - Rumen first-pass effects: orally administered drugs are exposed to ruminal microflora which may degrade (e.g., penicillin) or activate (e.g., some pro-drugs) them; large ruminal fluid volume dilutes oral doses; drugs may also be sequestered. Rumen motility affects absorption.\n4. PIGS - Oral bioavailability variations with feeding and slower gastric emptying; pigs metabolise some drugs more rapidly (e.g., faster clearance of some beta-lactams) and tolerate many drugs at mg/kg doses scaled metabolically; injection-site residues are a concern.\n(Also acceptable: birds - drug in drinking water; rabbits - extensive caecotrophy and poor oral dosing reliability.)\n(Any 3.)"
              },
              {
                "stem": "Define the withdrawal period of a veterinary medicine and explain why compliance is essential in dairy and beef production. (6 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Definition (2 marks):\nThe withdrawal period is the interval from the last administration of a veterinary medicine to the time when residues in edible products (meat, milk, eggs, honey) fall below the Maximum Residue Limit (MRL) established for that drug/species.\nWhy compliance is essential (4 marks, any four):\n1. Consumer safety: residues may cause toxicity, allergy or pharmacological effects (e.g., antimicrobial-induced dysbiosis).\n2. Prevention of antimicrobial resistance: sub-therapeutic residues select for resistant bacteria in the human food chain.\n3. Legal and regulatory compliance: selling products before the end of the withdrawal period is an offence; producers face penalties and loss of licences.\n4. Trade and market access: residue violations lead to rejection of consignments and loss of export markets.\n5. Dairy industry quality: antibiotic residues in milk spoil starter cultures for cheese and yoghurt fermentation.\n6. Public trust: repeated violations damage consumer confidence in animal products.\n(Any 4.)"
              },
              {
                "stem": "Classify the main anthelmintic drug groups used in ruminants, giving ONE example and the mechanism of action of each. (8 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Four groups (2 marks each = example + MOA):\n1. BENZIMIDAZOLES (e.g., albendazole, fenbendazole): bind beta-tubulin, inhibiting microtubule polymerisation and glucose uptake; nematodes starve and are expelled (ovicidal effect also useful).\n2. MACROCYCLIC LACTONES (e.g., ivermectin, doramectin, moxidectin): open glutamate-gated chloride channels, causing hyperpolarisation and flaccid paralysis of nematodes and arthropods.\n3. IMIDAZOTHIAZOLES/TETRAHYDROPYRIMIDINES (e.g., levamisole; pyrantel): nicotinic acetylcholine receptor agonists causing sustained depolarisation and spastic paralysis.\n4. SALICYLANILIDES/PHENOLS (e.g., closantel, nitroxynil; bithionol): uncouple oxidative phosphorylation, depleting ATP; effective against flukes and blood-feeding nematodes (Haemonchus).\n(Also acceptable: praziquantel - increases Ca2+ permeability of cestode/fluke tegument causing contraction and paralysis, mainly for tapeworms/flukes.)\n(Any 4 groups.)"
              },
              {
                "stem": "Describe the main classes of antimicrobials used in food animals with TWO examples each and explain how withdrawal times relate to responsible use. (8 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Classes with examples (6 marks, 2 each for three classes):\n1. BETA-LACTAMS (penicillin G procaine, amoxicillin, ceftiofur): cell wall synthesis inhibitors; short milk/meat withdrawal times; used for mastitis, respiratory and wound infections.\n2. TETRACYCLINES (oxytetracycline, chlortetracycline, doxycycline): 30S ribosomal inhibitors; broad spectrum; long-acting LA formulations; common in pneumonia and enteritis.\n3. MACROLIDES (tylosin, tilmicosin, tulathromycin): 50S inhibitors; used for respiratory disease in cattle, pigs and poultry; tilmicosin is highly toxic to humans (injection hazard).\n(Also acceptable: fluoroquinolones (enrofloxacin, danofloxacin) - DNA gyrase inhibitors, reserved for severe infections; aminoglycosides (gentamicin) - 30S inhibitors, narrow margin; sulphonamide/trimethoprim combinations - folate synthesis inhibitors.)\nResponsible use and withdrawal (2 marks):\nWithdrawal times are dose-, route- and species-specific and must be observed to keep residues below MRLs, prevent antimicrobial resistance and protect consumers. Drugs with long half-lives (e.g., some long-acting tetracyclines) need longer withdrawal. Never use banned drugs (chloramphenicol, nitroimidazoles) in food animals."
              },
              {
                "stem": "Compare the mechanisms of action and uses of fipronil, permethrin and amitraz in ectoparasite control. (6 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Each agent (2 marks):\n1. FIPRONIL - mechanism: blocks GABA-gated chloride channels in insects, causing hyperexcitation and death; use: spot-on for fleas and ticks in dogs and cats; also flea collars and environmental sprays. Long residual activity.\n2. PERMETHRIN (synthetic pyrethroid) - mechanism: delays closure of voltage-gated sodium channels, causing repeated firing, paralysis and death; use: flea/tick control in dogs, fly control in cattle (pour-on), sheep dips. HIGHLY TOXIC TO CATS (poor hepatic metabolism, no glucuronidation) - never use permethrin products on cats.\n3. AMITRAZ (formamidine) - mechanism: alpha-2 adrenergic agonist with monoamine oxidase inhibition, causing neuronal hyperexcitability and detachment of mites; use: dips/collars for generalised demodicosis in dogs. Side effects: sedation; reversal agent atipamezole; avoid in cats, diabetics and very young animals.\n(All 3 must be covered; each 2 marks.)"
              },
              {
                "stem": "What is extra-label (off-label) drug use in veterinary medicine, and what safeguards must the veterinary surgeon apply before using an extra-label drug in a food-producing animal in Kenya? (6 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "Definition (2 marks):\nExtra-label use is the administration of a registered veterinary (or human) medicine in a manner not stated on the label - different species, indication, dose, route or frequency - under a valid veterinarian-client-patient relationship (VCPR).\nSafeguards (4 marks, any four):\n1. VCPR: the veterinarian must have examined the animal(s) and have sufficient knowledge of the herd/flock.\n2. Withdrawal period extension: because tissue depletion in the target species is unstudied, the withdrawal period must be extended (a longer default interval is applied) to guarantee residue safety.\n3. No banned substances: extra-label use is prohibited for drugs banned in food animals (e.g., chloramphenicol, nitroimidazoles, some growth promoters).\n4. Record keeping: full records of drug, batch, dose, route, treated animals and withdrawal times must be kept and traceable.\n5. Client informed consent: the owner must be advised of the treatment and the extended withdrawal times.\n6. Safety and efficacy justification: no registered product is available for the condition/species, and the chosen drug has a documented pharmacological basis for use.\n(Any 4.)"
              }
            ]
