                "stem": "A 35-year-old patient is diagnosed with type II diabetes, hypertension and heart failure.\nPropose 4 drugs that you would recommend to be administered to this patient.(4 marks)\nDescribe the mode of action of the drugs mentioned in a) above.(8 marks)\nDescribe TWO (2) potential drug-drug interactions that you would be concerned about.(4 marks)",
                "modelAnswer": "TYPE II DIABETES + HYPERTENSION + HEART FAILURE (4 drugs; 4 + 8 + 4 marks):\na) FOUR RECOMMENDED DRUGS:\n1. Metformin (biguanide) — for glycemic control (avoid in decompensated HF/renal failure).\n2. Empagliflozin or dapagliflozin (SGLT-2 inhibitor) — glycemic control WITH proven heart-failure benefit.\n3. Enalapril or losartan (ACE inhibitor/ARB) — hypertension + HF (afterload reduction, remodeling).\n4. Furosemide (loop diuretic) — fluid overload in heart failure (and/or carvedilol — beta-blocker with HF indication).\nb) MODE OF ACTION OF EACH:\n- Metformin: activates AMPK → suppresses hepatic gluconeogenesis and increases peripheral insulin sensitivity (↑ glucose uptake in muscle) → lowers glucose without hypoglycemia.\n- Empagliflozin: inhibits SGLT-2 in the proximal renal tubule → blocks glucose reabsorption → glycosuria (lowers glucose, promotes weight loss); osmotic diuresis reduces preload → symptomatic and mortality benefit in HF.\n- Enalapril: inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme → ↓ angiotensin II → vasodilation and ↓ aldosterone → lowers BP, reduces afterload and cardiac remodeling (HF benefit).\n- Furosemide: loop diuretic inhibiting the Na-K-2Cl co-transporter in the thick ascending limb of Henle → powerful natriuresis/diuresis → ↓ preload, relieves congestion/edema in HF.\n- Carvedilol (if chosen): non-selective beta-blocker with alpha-1 blockade → ↓ heart rate, contractility and sympathetic drive → improves HF outcomes.\nc) TWO POTENTIAL DRUG-DRUG INTERACTIONS (4 marks):\n1. ACE inhibitor + potassium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone) or NSAIDs → HYPERKALEMIA (NSAIDs also reduce antihypertensive efficacy and worsen renal function).\n2. Loop diuretic (furosemide) + digoxin → diuretic-induced HYPOKALEMIA potentiates DIGOXIN TOXICITY (arrhythmias).\n3. SGLT-2 inhibitor + loop diuretics → additive dehydration, hypotension and acute kidney injury risk.\n4. Metformin + iodinated contrast → increased risk of lactic acidosis (withhold before contrast studies).\n(Any two.)"
